<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  fastdds_sim_cloud_pacing.xml

  Pace the simulated point-cloud writers so that a best-effort subscriber
  receives the clouds. Loaded by gazebo_sim.launch.py for the two kinds of
  process that publish them (the ros_gz bridge and gazebo_cloud_restamp), and
  by nothing else.

  THE PROBLEM, measured 2026-10-07 (docs/GAZEBO_SETUP.md 10.13)
  A 640x360 organised cloud is 3.7 MB. Fast DDS, here restricted to UDPv4
  (FASTDDS_BUILTIN_TRANSPORTS=UDPv4, which the compose files set because shared
  memory fails quietly: see docker-compose.mac.yml), sends it as a burst of
  datagrams back to back. That overflows the receiving socket's buffer (the
  kernel's RcvbufErrors counter rises), and losing one datagram loses the whole
  cloud for a best-effort reader. The competition's lane_segmentation is one: it
  subscribes to the cloud with qos_profile_sensor_data. With nothing changed, a best-effort subscriber
  to the re-stamped cloud saw 2.0 to 3.8 Hz while the node was publishing 7 to
  13 Hz, and the kernel counted 2198 receive-buffer errors in a 50 s window.
  The re-stamp node itself dropped nothing (clouds in = clouds out, per 10 s,
  logged).

  THE FIX
  A Fast DDS flow controller on exactly these writers: ASYNCHRONOUS publication,
  at most 131072 bytes every 1 ms (128 MB/s, about 2.5 times the 51 MB/s that
  13.7 Hz of clouds needs), so the same cloud arrives as a stream over about 30 ms instead
  of a burst. Measured with it: 13.55 Hz out of 13.7 Hz in (99%), 12 receive
  buffer errors in the same window. No camera rate, resolution or threshold was
  changed.

  WHAT IT TOUCHES
  Only writers named below. Every other writer in those two processes keeps the
  default (/clock, /scan, /odom, tf, the images). The launch sets
  RMW_FASTRTPS_PUBLICATION_MODE=AUTO for these two processes, which lets this
  file choose the mode per writer; without it the rmw forces SYNCHRONOUS and
  Fast DDS refuses to build a flow controller ("Cannot use a flow controller in
  synchronously publication mode").

  The profile names are topic names, written out for all three cameras so the
  file need not change with sim_cameras. If a topic is renamed in
  gazebo_bridge.yaml or the launch, rename it here too, or the cloud silently
  goes back to losing most of its frames. scripts/gazebo/cloud_rate_check.py,
  a best-effort count of the cloud against what the node received, is the
  check.
-->
<profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">

    <participant profile_name="igvc_sim_cloud_pacing" is_default_profile="true">
        <rtps>
            <flow_controller_descriptor_list>
                <flow_controller_descriptor>
                    <name>cloud_pacer</name>
                    <scheduler>FIFO</scheduler>
                    <max_bytes_per_period>131072</max_bytes_per_period>
                    <period_ms>1</period_ms>
                </flow_controller_descriptor>
            </flow_controller_descriptor_list>
        </rtps>
    </participant>

    <!-- bridge to re-stamp node: the private names gazebo_bridge.yaml publishes -->
    <data_writer profile_name="/sim/front_zed/points_optical_stamp">
        <qos>
            <publishMode>
                <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
                <flow_controller_name>cloud_pacer</flow_controller_name>
            </publishMode>
        </qos>
    </data_writer>
    <data_writer profile_name="/sim/left_zed/points_optical_stamp">
        <qos>
            <publishMode>
                <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
                <flow_controller_name>cloud_pacer</flow_controller_name>
            </publishMode>
        </qos>
    </data_writer>
    <data_writer profile_name="/sim/right_zed/points_optical_stamp">
        <qos>
            <publishMode>
                <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
                <flow_controller_name>cloud_pacer</flow_controller_name>
            </publishMode>
        </qos>
    </data_writer>

    <!-- re-stamp node to consumers: the ZED contract names -->
    <data_writer profile_name="/front_zed_camera_x/zed_node/point_cloud/cloud_registered">
        <qos>
            <publishMode>
                <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
                <flow_controller_name>cloud_pacer</flow_controller_name>
            </publishMode>
        </qos>
    </data_writer>
    <data_writer profile_name="/left_zed_camera_x/zed_node/point_cloud/cloud_registered">
        <qos>
            <publishMode>
                <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
                <flow_controller_name>cloud_pacer</flow_controller_name>
            </publishMode>
        </qos>
    </data_writer>
    <data_writer profile_name="/right_zed_camera_x/zed_node/point_cloud/cloud_registered">
        <qos>
            <publishMode>
                <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
                <flow_controller_name>cloud_pacer</flow_controller_name>
            </publishMode>
        </qos>
    </data_writer>

</profiles>
